<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thirty-Three Orientals</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Treinta y Tres Orientales</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Thirty-Three Orientals were a band of exiles from the Banda Oriental who, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-antonio-lavalleja">Juan Antonio Lavalleja</a>, crossed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buenos-aires">Buenos Aires</a> province in April 1825 to rise against Brazilian rule. Their landing sparked a revolt across the province, and a congress at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a> declared independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a> and union with the United Provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rio-de-la-plata">Río de la Plata</a> in August 1825. The resulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cisplatine-war">Cisplatine War</a> ended with Uruguayan independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thirty-three-orientals.jpg" alt="Thirty-Three Orientals" width="320" height="171" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Juan Manuel Blanes · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Manuel_Blanes_-_El_Juramento_de_los_Treinta_y_Tres_Orientales.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
